# Netlify redirects — evostr.com
# Place at the root of the publish directory. This site has no build step;
# the repo root is what gets deployed, so this file belongs here beside index.html.
#
# Rules are evaluated top to bottom; the first match wins.
# The trailing "!" forces the redirect even when a file exists at that path.
# It is required here because articles/5-signals-that-every-bpo-leader-needs-to-heed.html
# still exists on disk as a fallback stub, and without the force flag Netlify
# would serve that file instead of redirecting.


# --- 2026-09-01 · CXOutsourcers article consolidation -------------------------
# "5 Signals That Every BPO Leader Needs to Heed" (June 2026) covered the same
# panel and the same five signals as "What Enterprise Buyers Want from BPO
# Partners in 2026" (May 2026). The two pages were competing for the same
# queries, so the June content was merged into the May article.
#
# Both URL forms are redirected: the site serves .html and extensionless
# variants of every page, so both need a rule or one form keeps returning 200.

/articles/5-signals-that-every-bpo-leader-needs-to-heed.html   /articles/what-enterprise-buyers-want-2026.html   301!
/articles/5-signals-that-every-bpo-leader-needs-to-heed        /articles/what-enterprise-buyers-want-2026.html   301!


# --- Optional: single-URL canonicalization ----------------------------------
# Not enabled. Today every page answers on both /page and /page.html with
# identical content, while canonical tags and sitemap.xml both declare the
# .html form. The redirects above therefore target .html, so nothing points at
# a URL that declares itself non-canonical.
#
# The 2026-09-01 audit recommends collapsing to one form. If you adopt
# extensionless URLs, uncomment the rule below AND update all 55 canonical
# tags plus sitemap.xml in the same deploy — doing one without the other
# creates a redirect loop or a canonical conflict.
